From: substrate
Subject: kdrive: fix off-by-one in KdUnregisterFd array compaction

KdUnregisterFd removes an input-fd handler from kdInputFds[] and then
compacts the array.  The shift loop bound was off by one:

    kdNumInputFds--;
    for (j = i; j < (kdNumInputFds - 1); j++)   /* stops one short */
        kdInputFds[j] = kdInputFds[j + 1];

After the decrement, moving entries [i+1 .. old-1] into [i .. old-2]
requires j to run i .. kdNumInputFds-1.  The `- 1` stopped one early,
so when a NON-last entry was removed the final handler was never
shifted down -- it stayed in the array as a stale duplicate pointing at
the device that was just disabled/freed.

This bites on X server reset.  In the display-manager flow the greeter
(sgreet) is the sole client; when it disconnects at login the server
regenerates: every input device is disabled (KdUnregisterFd, fd closed,
driverPrivate NULLed) and re-enabled as a brand-new KdPointerInfo on the
same reused fd.  The off-by-one left the OLD pointer's handler in
kdInputFds[], so KdProcessInputEvents kept invoking EvdevPtrRead with the
stale KdPointerInfo.  Its driverPrivate was NULL, so EvdevPtrRead's
defensive guard drained-and-discarded every report -- the mouse froze the
instant the window manager came up, which had been worked around with
-dumbSched and the driverPrivate junk-pointer guard.

Use the correct bound `j < kdNumInputFds`.

--- a/hw/kdrive/src/kinput.c
+++ b/hw/kdrive/src/kinput.c
@@ -221,7 +221,20 @@
             if (do_close)
                 close(kdInputFds[i].fd);
             kdNumInputFds--;
-            for (j = i; j < (kdNumInputFds - 1); j++)
+            /* Shift the tail down over the removed slot.  Bound must be
+             * the (already-decremented) count: to move entries
+             * [i+1 .. old_count-1] into [i .. old_count-2] we iterate
+             * j = i .. kdNumInputFds-1 and copy [j+1].  The old
+             * `j < kdNumInputFds - 1` stopped one short, leaving the
+             * last handler un-shifted -- a stale duplicate that still
+             * pointed at a freed/disabled device.  On X server reset
+             * (the greeter client disconnecting) that stale entry kept
+             * firing EvdevPtrRead with the old KdPointerInfo whose
+             * driverPrivate had been NULLed, so every pointer read hit
+             * the "junk driverPrivate" guard and DRAINED all mouse
+             * motion -- the cursor froze the moment the window manager
+             * connected. */
+            for (j = i; j < kdNumInputFds; j++)
                 kdInputFds[j] = kdInputFds[j + 1];
             break;
         }
